preferred-citation:
  type: article
  title: >-
    Analysis of acoustic fluctuations for 150-km, low frequency transmissions
    across the Gulf Stream in the vicinity of the New England Seamount chain
  authors:
    - family-names: Ragland
      given-names: John
    - family-names: Colosi
      given-names: John
    - family-names: Hoekstra
      given-names: Katherine
    - family-names: Dzieciuch
      given-names: Matthew
    - family-names: Alford
      given-names: Matthew
    - family-names: Bellerjeau
      given-names: Charlotte
  year: 2026
  month: 7
  publisher:
    name: ESS Open Archive
  doi: 10.22541/essoar.15006016/v1
  url: "https://essopenarchive.org/doi/full/10.22541/essoar.15006016/v1"
  notes: Preprint, submitted to the Journal of the Acoustical Society of America.
  languages:
    - en
  abstract: >-
    Results are presented from the 2023 pilot study of the New England Seamount
    Calibrated Acoustic Fluctuation Experiment (NESCAFE23), designed to quantify
    acoustic transmission variability across the Gulf Stream near the Atlantis
    II seamount complex. Western boundary current systems such as the Gulf
    Stream present a challenging environment for ocean acoustics, where three
    distinct water masses co-mingle within a compact, dynamic frontal region.
    In late April 2023, two acoustic transceivers (SION and SIOE) were deployed
    153 km apart and exchanged hourly 250 Hz broadband transmissions over 24
    hours before SIOE was lost to mooring blowdown. Twenty-three receptions
    between 205–295 Hz were recorded on two vertical sub-arrays spanning
    1100–850 m and 340–90 m depth. The acoustic path directly transected the
    Gulf Stream front, introducing strong horizontal sound-speed and density
    structure. Collocated temperature and salinity measurements show that the
    internal-wave field near the receiver is consistent with a Garrett–Munk
    spectrum at standard energy levels. Vertical spectra of phase and
    log-amplitude, and vertical coherence, are compared with weak-fluctuation
    and path-integral predictions, showing partial agreement but sensitivity to
    fine-scale frontal structure and multi-path interference. Observed
    intensity distributions exhibit glinting and fade-outs indicative of near
    partially saturated propagation.
